# TrendingCompanyResearchList: BVC Companies, Window 20–27 Oct 2026

**Scope note:** The brief asked for trending companies in the 20–27 October 2026 window. My searches confirmed the financial figures below, but most articles returned without a verifiable publication date, so I could not confirm that coverage clusters inside the window. Where a date is missing, I say so. Dated price points are listed separately at the end.

---

## Entry 1: Organización Terpel S.A. (BVC: TERPEL)

**name:** Organización Terpel S.A. (BVC: TERPEL)

**market_position:**
- **Business and fit:** Fuel and energy distribution. This is the closest match to the wholesale brief of the three companies.
- **Reputation claim:** Terpel says it is "la compañía de distribución de combustibles con mejor reputación según el Ranking Merco Empresas 2025" (terpel.com, undated homepage; the Merco ranking year is 2025).
- **Q2 2026 results:** Consolidated revenue of COP 10.5 trillion, +12.9% YoY. EBITDA of COP 509 billion, +17.8% YoY. Net income +24% YoY. Source: Aval Casa de Bolsa results note on Terpel's 2T26 report (Facebook/LinkedIn post; publication date not shown in retrieved text, but it covers 2T26, i.e., published after June 2026).
- **H1 2026 results:** Net income of COP 409,254 million, +25.4% vs. H1 2025. Sources: Portafolio and La República (articles on H1 2026 results; dates not shown in retrieved text).
- **Q1 2026 results:** Net income of COP 211,789 million, +26.9% YoY. Source: Valora Analitik (social post; date not shown in retrieved text). Aval Casa de Bolsa reported Q1 EBITDA of COP 529 billion, +21% YoY (X post; date not shown).
- **Margin context:** Net margin of 1.77% (Simply Wall St, last earnings update 30 Mar 2026). This is a low-margin business.
- **Key contracts:**
  - Ecopetrol supply contract: about US$2.6 billion, 1 Apr 2026 to 28 Feb 2027, 11 months (BNAmericas; Infobae article dated 31 Mar 2026 reports the same contract).
  - Avianca jet-fuel supply: extended by one year to September 2027 (dataiFX; article date not verified). The brief's start date of 1 Oct 2026 is not confirmed by my search.
- **Competitive analysis:** The retrieved material does not include a peer comparison (for example, against other Colombian fuel distributors). I cannot make a competitive-share claim from verified sources.

**future_outlook:**
- **Growth drivers (reported):** Q2 2026 growth was attributed to EDS (service stations) and lubricants in Colombia (Aval Casa de Bolsa, 2T26 note). The company also launched Terpel GT Premium at the start of 2026 (Terpel 1T26 results PDF).
- **Contract visibility:** The Ecopetrol and Avianca contracts provide volume visibility through early-to-mid 2027.
- **Headwinds:** Aval Casa de Bolsa noted that the Q1 EBITDA growth was partly offset by a "decalaje" (price-lag) effect, with underlying performance about -1% excluding that effect. Fuel price regulation and margin pressure are standard sector headwinds, but I did not verify a specific 2026 regulatory action.
- **Forward calendar:** Not verified.

**investment_potential:**
- **Bull case:** Earnings grew double digits in Q1 and Q2 2026. EBITDA and net income both rose in the most recent reported quarter. Contract coverage with Ecopetrol and Avianca supports near-term volumes. The company holds a reputational lead in fuel distribution (Merco 2025, self-reported).
- **Bear case:** Net margin is very thin (1.77%). Part of the Q1 EBITDA growth was a timing effect that reversed when excluded. Revenue depends heavily on a few large contracts, including Ecopetrol, which is a state-linked counterparty whose terms can change. The stock price has been volatile across sources (see price section).
- **Main risk:** Margin compression in fuel distribution, driven by pricing, timing effects, or contract renewal terms (the Ecopetrol contract ends 28 Feb 2027).
- **Suitability:** The fundamentals are the strongest of the three companies in this list, but the margin profile and contract concentration require the investor to accept sector-specific risk.

